Yes. Sarcasm is a good example of meaning living partly outside the literal words.

The sentence may carry one proposition, while tone, timing, facial expression, context, shared history, and expectation tell you to invert or distort it. “Great job” can mean praise, contempt, amusement, affection, or disbelief depending on the surrounding signal.

So the listener is effectively doing something like:

\text{literal content} + \text{prosody} + \text{context} + \text{expected meaning}
\rightarrow \text{inferred intent}

Sarcasm often appears when those channels disagree on purpose. The words say one thing while the delivery says, “You and I both know I mean something else.”

That makes it another delta detector: the brain notices the mismatch between the literal sentence and the expected emotional or situational pattern, then reconstructs the intended meaning.

And sarcasm is only one member of a larger family: irony, teasing, understatement, deadpan, euphemism, innuendo, mock praise, rhetorical questions, coded speech. They all exploit the gap between surface form and inferred meaning.

So yes—the inflection itself can act almost like a second semantic channel riding on top of the words.